  "title": "National Minimum Wage and Living Wage: accommodation: Accommodation rates - GOV.UK",
  "content": [
    {
      "p": "Accommodation provided by an employer can be taken into account when calculating the National Minimum Wage or National Living Wage ."
    },
    {
      "p": "No other kind of company benefit (such as food, a car, childcare vouchers) counts towards the minimum wage."
    },
    {
      "h2": "Accommodation offset rates"
    },
    {
      "p": "The accommodation rates are set in April each year."
    },
    {
      "p": "From April 2026, the rates are:"
    },
    {
      "li": "£11.10 (daily rate)"
    },
    {
      "li": "£77.70 (weekly rate)"
    },
    {
      "h2": "Previous rates"
    },
    {
      "table": [
        ["Year", "Daily accommodation offset rate", "Weekly accommodation offset rate"],
        ["2025", "£10.66", "£74.62"],
        ["2024", "£9.99", "£69.93"],
        ["2023", "£9.10", "£63.70"],
        ["2022", "£8.70", "£60.90"],
        ["2021", "£8.36", "£58.52"],
        ["2020", "£8.20", "£57.40"],
        ["2019", "£7.55", "£52.85"],
        ["2018", "£7", "£49"],
        ["2017", "£6.40", "£44.80"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "p": "Use the minimum wage calculator to check if it’s been paid."
    },
    {
      "h2": "Using the offset rate to work out the minimum wage"
    },
    {
      "p": "If an employer charges more than the offset rate, the difference is taken off the worker’s pay which counts for the National Minimum Wage or National Living Wage."
    },
    {
      "p": "This means the higher the accommodation charge, the lower a worker’s pay when calculating the minimum wage."
    },
    {
      "p": "If the accommodation charge is at or below the offset rate, it does not have an effect on the worker’s pay."
    },
    {
      "p": "If the accommodation is free, the offset rate is added to the worker’s pay."
    },
    {
      "p": "See examples of accommodation rates for minimum wage calculations ."
    }
  ],
